and may not be distributed outside the prospective employer.

3. Absolute Prohibition on Commercial Use

Except for the limited employment-evaluation use expressly permitted by
Section 1(d), commercial use is prohibited. "Commercial use" means any use that
is intended for, directed toward, connected with, or reasonably anticipated to
result in commercial advantage, business benefit, monetary compensation, or
revenue, whether directly or indirectly and whether or not any revenue or
profit is actually received. A prospective employer's evaluation is not
commercial use solely because the employer is a for-profit organization, but
any use beyond Section 1(d) is commercial use.

Prohibited commercial use includes, without limitation:

  a. use by or for a for-profit company or other commercial organization,
     except as expressly permitted by Section 1(d);
  b. internal business use, evaluation, benchmarking, or proof-of-concept work,
     except as expressly permitted by Section 1(d);
  c. commercial research and development or preparation for commercialization;
  d. use in consulting, contracting, employment, or services performed for a
     commercial client or sponsor;
  e. incorporation into, or use to develop, test, manufacture, market, support,
     or provide any commercial product, service, or hardware;
  f. sale, rental, licensing for a fee, paid access, or paid distribution;
  g. use in advertising, fundraising, investment solicitation, or a monetized
     project; and
  h. any activity for which compensation or commercial benefit is received or
     expected.

Normal tuition, academic salaries, scholarships, and public or nonprofit
research grants do not by themselves make an otherwise permitted educational
or academic use commercial. However, work performed for the commercial benefit
of a for-profit sponsor, employer, client, or partner is always prohibited.

If a use is both educational, academic, or hobby-related and commercial, the
commercial prohibition controls and the use is not permitted.

